  {
    "episode_id": 70863,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70863.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Well, a couple things. One, I think the center of gravity of our politics is moving much, much more local. If the economy is global, all politics is a local job. When you think about where you live, you work, you play, where you're going to raise your kids, and then the things that you rely on, transportation, schools. parks, libraries, safety. Those are all services that are delivered by local government. And this is not the first time we've ever kind of been where the lead is happening more locally than nationally, but what's different is not only those innovations in those specific areas of parks, libraries, or education, and I can talk about a little more about that. But cities then are taking on things that used to be totally the responsibility of the national government. Take something I'm having to be in New York when you interview me. I did this the other day in Chicago. But Mayor Bloomberg and I, I learned it from him. push new research centers. He created the research center with Cornell down at Roosevelt Island on Roosevelt called the 78. We push what is called the Discovery Center with the University of Illinois's Computer Science and Engineering School. The whole idea of a new research center of a university, that would have been a national or state function. It's become a city. Climate change. Chicago had the only major city with coal plants in it. We shut those down. Those are supposed to be the policies in the national government. We were taking on that responsibility.",
