  {
    "episode_id": 70863,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70863.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, two things I'm not, let me at least challenge the premise. I'm not, I'm not, I don't think you're, I think you're accurate without being a hundred percent accurate. I mean, the largest caucus inside the House of Representatives are what are called the new Democrats, which are direct ideological political lineage from Bill Clinton. or the new deal, new, what would be called new Democrats, DLC people. So that's a, B, I think we're center left rather than just left. C, you can't separate from policy from politics or the other way around. Bill Clinton and Barack Obama in both their election and their re-election ran the same playbook. In 2006 when I was chair of the DCCC and we took back the House and the six-year Bush's tenure and in 2018 when the Democrats took back the House. So you've had six elections under one model where the Democrats have made unbelievable gains either at the White House level or the congressional level. And to me, there is no reason to throw that successful playbook out. I mean, right after 2018, where you had Democrats running on preexisting condition and controlling healthcare costs, we're having this massive debate about Medicare for all, and none of it has to deal with cost control. of it has to deal with the fact that your kids need healthcare and your parents who are now living in the basement also need healthcare, and you're getting squeezed on the cost. And we just, and here's the thing, elections are supposed to be models of how to then govern. And we took this model and immediately seeing that we won seats that we flipped from red to blue, some of the biggest gains that happened since water, the Watergate class of 74, and we throw it out. It's not, and we're advocating Getting rid of the Affordable Care Act and replacing it with something that can't happen and shouldn't happen. And to me, and I would say to you, if we're going to win, you can either go learn the lesson in 92 and 96, learn the lesson in 2006, learn the lesson in 2008, learn the lesson in 2012, learn the lesson in 2018. And let's not forget it between 2008 and 2012 when President Obama gets elected, he gets reelected. It was in that time that Bernie Sanders wanted to challenge him. Didn't think he was doing what he wanted to do from quote unquote, big P progressive politics. And",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
